# Content-hashed and immutable assets. These were being revalidated on every
# navigation because nothing set a max-age.
#
# There is deliberately NO /* rule here: Cloudflare merges a matching catch-all
# into the specific rule, so "max-age=0, must-revalidate" from /* landed in front
# of "max-age=31536000, immutable" and browsers honoured the first one. Verified
# live: /og-default.jpg was still returning max-age=0. HTML keeps Cloudflare's
# own default, which is already revalidate-always.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

/wall/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/og-default.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# The public crowd API. Headers set on the Response inside a PRERENDERED Astro
# endpoint are thrown away — the route is written to disk as a plain file at
# build time and the assets layer decides what to send. Verified live on
# 2026-08-25: /api/crowd.json returned "max-age=0, must-revalidate" and NO
# Access-Control-Allow-Origin, despite both being set in crowd.json.ts.
#
# That silent loss matters more here than anywhere else on the site: without
# CORS a browser-side integration fails outright, and the whole reason this
# endpoint exists is that someone asked to integrate it.
#
# Wide-open origin is correct — every number in the file is already public on
# the page it came from, and there is no auth to leak. A day of caching matches
# the refresh cadence (venues are re-measured over weeks, not hours).
/api/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
